Syntax: trap <direction> <trap type>         (e.g. "trap north fire")
        trap <door> [direction] <trap type>  (named doors, e.g. "trap gate fire")
        trap <portal> <trap type>            (magical portals)

The trap type is always the last word of the command; everything before it
names what you are trapping.

Door traps may be placed on closed doors, gates, or other barriers along the
direction of travel.  They are easy to set (and relatively easy to detect).
Door traps are triggered when the exit is opened.  They do a moderate amount of
damage to the victim opening them, and may also lightly harm others in the
vicinity.  This skill also governs trapping magical portals, which are set the
same way and triggered when someone enters them.

Set traps allows a skillful looter from dissuading an overeager pursuer from
following or to protect the looter's booty.  Traps come in several varieties:
door (and portal) traps, container traps, arrow traps, land mines, and
grenades.

All traps take time to set.  Improper training may cause the trap to backfire
on the looter during the trapping process.  The damage the trap will do depends
on the trapper's level, the trapper's skill, the variety of trap, and the trap
type.  <trap type>'s are located in a separate help file.

Note: "damage" as used below is a generic term.  For trap types that don't do
physical harm (sleep, poison, etc), greater "damage" reflects an increased
chance to be affected by the trap.  eg.  A higher immunity to poison would be
needed to dodge the effects of a "high damage" poison trap.  Obviously,
everyone that doesn't save, will be affected by the sleep, poison, etc.
equally.

